   173	is a function of 𝒯_j: FACTOR_U is Las Vegas and its *output* (the factorisation of |u₀|, |v₀|) is a function of T alone,
   174	its running time a function of T and its own coins; acceptance is a function of the factorisations, the cached atoms and
   175	the SQ coins. Hence, conditional on 𝒯_j, the labels b_i of the accepted rows of round j are i.i.d. uniform on Z/n. ∎
   176	(This is [DL] Lemma T_p with the shape-test coins added to 𝒯_j; the recursive structure inside FACTOR_U is irrelevant,
   177	because only its output enters the row and that output is deterministic.)
   178	
   179	**Lemma Sel-DL (cost of selection and rounds; proved).** Let p_sel be the probability that one trial (SAMPLE_FIELD +
   180	DESCEND_ONCE) returns a row, C_trial a bound on the conditional expected cost of one trial or one DESCEND_ONCE given the
   181	past, and T_post a bound on the expected cost of EXACT_DEPENDENCE_U. Then
   182	  E[cost of DLOG25] ≤ (1 + log₂ n)·((M_DL + 1)·C_trial/p_sel + T_post + O(M_DL log² p)) + T_S,
   183	where T_S = B^{1+o(1)} is the cost of S (Lemma CS), and p_sel ≥ (γ/4)(γ/(16 log p))^{2k}/(32k²).
   184	*Proof.* p_sel: [U] select-U(i) with K-U for DL; there are no factor exits. Selection: Wald, ≤ C_trial/p_sel. Kept field:
   185	size-biased law, E[1/ς_{A*}] ≤ 1/p_sel ([U] select-U(ii), verbatim). A round in field A costs ≤ M_DL·C_trial/ς_A + T_post
   186	+ O(M_DL log² p) (labels, exponentiations). By Lemmas T_p-U and HX, each round succeeds with conditional probability
   187	≥ φ(n)/n ≥ 1/(1 + log₂ n) given all earlier rounds, so the number of rounds is dominated by a geometric variable with
   188	mean ≤ 1 + log₂ n; Wald's identity (each round's conditional expected cost is bounded as above given A) gives the
   189	bound. ∎
   190	
   191	## 4. Proposition XK at growing degree (Proposition XK-U; proved)
   192	
   193	Fix a kept field A: f irreducible of odd degree d with content 1, 0 < |f_d| < p, m a simple root of f mod p, and
   194	‖f‖_∞ ≤ 3MH ([pp] Lemma GB_w with (P2)). Rows i = 1..M, M = M_DL, with labels. Notation of [DL] §4: K = Q(α), t = f_d,
   195	𝔞 = O_K + αO_K, 𝔍_P = (a − bα)𝔞^{−1}, Λ₁, γ_x, y_P(x), Λ₂.
   196	
   197	**What is degree-free.** Lemmas XK0(i)–(ii), XK1, XK2, XK3, XK5 and HX of [DL] contain no constant depending on d:
   198	their proofs are pure algebra (Gauss's content lemma, Dedekind–Kummer, the reduction map at the degree-one prime
   199	𝔭 = (p, α − m), saturation of kernels, μ_K = {±1} for d odd). Their numerical hypotheses hold in the U design:
   200	- |F_A(P)| ≤ X_F < p and p ∤ t, so every a − bα is a 𝔭-unit (N𝔍_P < p);
   201	- |G(P)| ≤ 2MV < p, g₁ < p, and all q ≤ B are < p, so (2.1) holds in F_p^*;
   202	- d is odd ([U] §1: d is the least odd integer ≥ max(3, d₀D^{1/5})), so W(x) = ±1 on Λ₂.
   203	Columns: one per q ≤ B, one for g₁, and one per prime ideal above some r ≤ B that occurs; at most d per r, so
   204	C_col ≤ (d + 1)π(B) + 1 = C̄ = L^{a+o(1)} (d ≤ log p). Rank Λ₂ ≥ M − C_col − r_U ≥ 1 for M = C̄ + d.
   205	
   206	**Lemma XK0-U (bad primes; proved).** The primes r ≤ B with r | t·disc(f) number ≤ log₂|t·disc f| = O(log p), because
   207	log|disc f| ≤ d log d + (2d − 2) log(√(d + 1)·3MH) = O(d(μ + h) log p + d log d) = O(log p) (d(μ + h) = O(dθ) = O(1)).
   208	For each, an r-maximal order, the primes above r and the valuations v_𝔯(𝔍_P) are computed in expected time
   209	poly(d, log p) (Round 2 at a known prime, Buchmann–Lenstra 1994; decomposition and valuations, Cohen GTM 138,
   210	Algs. 6.1.8, 6.2.9, 4.8.17; the only randomness is Cantor–Zassenhaus factoring over F_r, and r ≤ B; v_r(disc f) =
   211	O(log p) bounds the r-adic precision). Finding them: t·disc f mod r for all r ≤ B, Õ(dB). Total O(log p)·poly(d, log p)
   212	plus Õ(dB) = L^{a+o(1)}. ∎
   213	
   214	**Lemma XK4-U (unit elimination at growing degree; proved).** Let s := r₁ + r₂ ≤ d, r_U := s − 1, and x₁, …, x_s ∈ Λ₁
   215	independent with entries of at most β_x bits. One finds a nonzero n ∈ Z^s with Σ n_jx_j ∈ Λ₂ in time
   216	poly(d)·Õ(M·(P + β_x) + κ²) with κ, P = O(dβ_x + d² + d·log(kMd log p)).
   217	*Proof (every d-dependence explicit).*
   218	- **Conjugate bounds.** For an atom P = (a, b) (|a|, |b| ≤ V, F(a, b) ≠ 0) and every embedding σ:
   219	  |σ(a − bα)| ≤ U := V(2 + 3MH) < p (Cauchy: |σα| ≤ 1 + ‖f‖_∞/|t| ≤ 1 + 3MH; and 8MHV ≤ X_F < p), and
   220	  |σ(a − bα)| = |N(a − bα)|/∏_{σ′≠σ}|σ′(a − bα)| ≥ (|F(a, b)|/|t|)·U^{−(d−1)} ≥ p^{−d}. So every coordinate of
   221	  Log(a − bα) has absolute value ≤ 2d log p, and ‖Log(a − bα)‖₂ ≤ 2d^{3/2} log p.
   222	- **Gap.** Blanksby–Montgomery (Acta Arith. 18 (1971) 355–369): a nonzero algebraic integer of degree d′ that is not a
   223	  root of unity has Mahler measure > 1 + 1/(52d′ log(6d′)). For a unit z ∈ K of degree d′ | d,
   224	  log M(z) = (d′/d)Σ_σ log⁺|σz| ≤ d′·‖Log z‖_∞ ≤ d‖Log z‖_∞ (complex coordinates carry the factor 2). So every
   225	  nonzero w ∈ Λ_U := Log(O_K^×) has ‖w‖₂ ≥ ‖w‖_∞ ≥ β_d := log(1 + 1/(52d log 6d))/d ≥ 1/(105d² log 6d), and Log z = 0
   226	  iff z ∈ μ_K (Kronecker).
   227	- **Small relations exist.** v_j := Log γ_{x_j} = Σ_P y_P(x_j) Log(a_P − b_Pα) ∈ Λ_U, with
   228	  ‖v_j‖₂ ≤ 2^{β_x}·2kM·2d^{3/2} log p (≤ 2kM atom occurrences, each |coefficient| ≤ 2^{β_x}). Fix (for the proof only) an
   229	  LLL-reduced basis u₁, …, u_{r_U} of Λ_U: ‖u_i*‖ ≥ 2^{−(i−1)/2}λ₁(Λ_U) ≥ 2^{−r_U/2}β_d. Back-substitution with
   230	  |μ_{ij}| ≤ 1/2 gives integer coordinates |c_{ji}| ≤ (3/2)^{r_U}·2^{r_U/2}‖v_j‖₂/β_d =: C₁ (the fixed-d text [DL] wrote
   231	  2^{O(d²)}; 2^{O(d)} suffices, and either is polynomial in bit length). The r_U × s integer matrix (c_{ji}) has a
   232	  nonzero kernel vector n₀ with ‖n₀‖_∞ ≤ R := max(1, r_U!·C₁^{r_U}) (Cramer on a maximal nonsingular minor; if all v_j = 0,
   233	  n₀ = e₁). Thus
   234	    log₂R ≤ r_U log₂ r_U + r_U(r_U log₂(3/2) + r_U/2 + β_x + log₂(4kMd^{3/2} log p) + log₂(105d² log 6d))
   235	          = O(dβ_x + d² + d log(kMd log p)).
   236	- **LLL with the explicit precision.** b_j := (e_j, ⌊2^κ v_j⌉) ∈ Z^{s + s}, coordinates within 1 of 2^κv_j. If n ∈ Rel,
   237	  ‖Σn_jb_j‖ ≤ (1 + s√s)‖n‖; if n ∉ Rel, ‖Σn_jb_j‖ ≥ 2^κβ_d − s√s‖n‖. LLL's first vector has norm ≤ R′ :=
   238	  2^{(s−1)/2}(1 + s√s)R (the lattice contains Σ n₀,jb_j), so its coefficient vector has norm ≤ R′; with
   239	  2^κβ_d > (1 + s√s)R′ it lies in Rel, and it is nonzero. So κ := ⌈log₂((1 + s√s)R′/β_d)⌉ + 1 = log₂R + O(d + log d·…)
   240	  = O(dβ_x + d² + d log(kMd log p)).
   241	- **Precision ledger.** Each coordinate of each Log(a_P − b_Pα) to absolute precision 2^{−P}, P := κ + β_x +
   242	  log₂(2kM) + O(log d), suffices for rounding, accumulation and certification ([DL] Lemma XK4 ledger, with the sum over
   243	  ≤ 2kM occurrences and s ≤ d coordinates). By the conjugate lower bound, absolute precision P + log₂V + d log₂p + O(1)
   244	  on the roots of f gives this (relative error of σ(a − bα) ≤ V·ε·p^d). Root approximation of a degree-d integer
   245	  polynomial with O(log p)-bit coefficients to b bits costs poly(d, log p, b) (Schönhage 1982; Pan, J. Symbolic Comput.
   246	  33 (2002) 701–733).
   247	- **Cost.** Roots: poly(d, log p, P). Logs of ≤ 2kM atoms in s coordinates: d·2kM·Õ(P). The sums: Õ(dM(P + β_x)).
   248	  LLL (Nguyen–Stehlé L², SIAM J. Comput. 39 (2009) 874–903) in dimension ≤ 2d with O(κ)-bit entries: poly(d)·Õ(κ²).
   249	- **Certification.** Accept n only if ‖Σn_jv_j‖_∞ < β_d/2 is certified by ball arithmetic: a true relation passes by the
   250	  precision, a non-relation cannot pass by the gap. ∎
   251	
   252	**Proposition XK-U (proved).** EXACT_DEPENDENCE_U = [DL] EXACT_DEPENDENCE with Lemmas XK0-U, XK4-U and M = M_DL is Las
   253	Vegas, outputs a primitive x* ∈ Λ₂ and λ = log_g W(x*) ∈ {0, n/2}, and has expected cost L^{3a+o(1)} (L = L_p[2/5]).
   254	*Proof.* Correctness: [DL] Lemmas XK1–XK5 (degree-free) with XK0-U, XK4-U. Cost, step by step:
   255	- E exactly: each row has ≤ 2k(1 + log₂(2MV)) + 1 + 2k·log₂X_F ≤ 5k log₂ p nonzero entries, each of absolute value
   256	  ≤ 2k log₂ p (k ≤ log p); the valuations are read off the factorisations of G(P) and F(P) (already computed by the
   257	  batch smoothness of SQ_SEARCH) plus XK0-U. Cost M·poly(k, d, log p) + L^{a+o(1)}.
   258	- Rank profile modulo a random prime q ∈ [Q, 2Q], Q = M(log p)³: a fixed maximal nonsingular minor has
   259	  log|det| ≤ ρ·log(√(5k log p)·2k log p) = O(ρ log log p) (Hadamard; k, d ≤ log p), so ≤ O(ρ log log p/log Q) primes in
   260	  [Q, 2Q] divide it, against ≫ Q/log Q primes there: Pr[the minor survives] ≥ 1/2. Dense elimination: O(M·C̄·ρ) = L^{3a+o(1)}.
   261	- Dixon p-adic lifting for d right-hand sides: Õ(dρ³) = L^{3a+o(1)}; outputs β_x = O(ρ log log p) = L^{a+o(1)} bits.
   262	- Verification x_iᵀE = 0: nnz(E)·d·β_x = L^{2a+o(1)}; a failure resamples q (expected ≤ 2 restarts, [DL]).
   263	- Lemma XK4-U with this β_x: κ, P = O(dβ_x + d² + d log(kMd log p)) = L^{a+o(1)}, cost L^{2a+o(1)}.
   264	- W(x*), A*, B*: M exponentiations with L^{a+o(1)}-bit exponents, L^{2a+o(1)}.
   265	Every factor poly(d, k, log p) is (log p)^{O(1)} = L^{o(1)} (in fact exp(O(log log p)) = exp(o(Λ))). ∎
   266	
   267	So the answer to "does the cost stay L^{o(1)} relative to L[2/5]": yes. The growing degree costs only poly(d) factors
   268	and O(d)-fold larger bit lengths (κ ≍ dβ_x instead of β_x), never an exponent. The cubic linear algebra L^{3a} is the
   269	only post-processing term that is not L^{2a}; its effect is computed in §6.
   270	
   271	## 5. Shape test, recursion, and why DL has no log recursion
   272	
   273	- **The shape test is factoring.** DESCEND_ONCE needs the complete factorisations of |u₀|, |v₀| ≤ √(2p). DLOG25 calls
   274	  FACTOR_U[τ′] on each, with fresh coins. By Theorem U, for every integer m ≤ √(2p), E[cost of FACTOR_U[τ′](m)] ≤
   275	  F_U(√(2p)) ≤ L_{√(2p)}[2/5, c_U(τ′) + o(1)] ≤ e·L_p[2/5, 2^{−2/5}c_U(τ′) + o(1)] (the scaling step of [U] Lemma F_α:
   276	  Λ(√(2p)) ≤ 2^{−2/5}Λ(p) + o(1)). This bound is uniform in m, and conditional on the past (fresh coins), so it is a valid
   277	  C_trial term in Lemma Sel-DL ([rec] Lemma W covers adaptively chosen sub-calls).
   278	- **No recursion on logarithms.** The logarithms of the halves u₀, v₀ are logarithms in the same group F_p^*, so a
   279	  recursive DL call would be on the same p: no size reduction. The only recursion is inside FACTOR_U (Theorem U's own,
   280	  via Lemma F_α). DLOG25 itself is one level.
   281	- **Why the constant is still the factoring constant.** See Lemma C25 (§6): a one-level outer loop around a factoring
   282	  subroutine with constant c* costs ≥ min(c_U(τ), c*) for the same reason that the fixed point of Lemma F_α is c_U.
   283	
   284	## 6. Theorem DL25 and the cost (proved from U)
   285	
   286	**Theorem DL25.** Fix rationals a, κ, d₀, a′, κ′, d₀′ > 0. DLOG25[τ; τ′] is Las Vegas, and for every prime p and
   287	primitive root g it returns log_g h mod (p − 1) in expected time L_p[2/5, c_DL(τ; τ′) + o(1)], with
   288	  c_DL(τ; τ′) = max( 3a,  a + s + max(a, σ),  a + s + 2^{−2/5}c_U(τ′) ),  s = 2κ(1 + C_γ(τ)),  σ = 2θ₀(d₀, κ)/(5a). (6.1)
   289	*Proof.*
   290	- **Correctness.** Every output is verified (g^X = h); by Lemmas XK1–XK5, HX and T_p-U, A* + XB* ≡ λ (mod n) for the
   291	  true X, so a round with gcd(B*, n) = 1 returns X. The alternation with exhaustive search makes the algorithm
   292	  terminate for small p (where structural guards may fail) and costs a factor ≤ 2.
   293	- **Cost.** Lemma Sel-DL with:
   294	  - 1/p_sel ≤ 4γ^{−1}(16 log p/γ)^{2k}·32k² = L^{s+o(1)} (γ ≥ (log p)^{−C_γ}, 2k log log p = 2κΛ(1 + o(1)); [U] (10.1));
   295	  - M_DL = (d + 1)π(B) + 1 + d = L^{a+o(1)};
   296	  - C_trial ≤ 2F_U(√(2p)) + 2k·T_SQ + poly(d, log p), T_SQ = L^{max(a,σ)+o(1)} ([U] §10), so
   297	    C_trial ≤ L^{max(2^{−2/5}c_U(τ′), max(a, σ))+o(1)};
   298	  - T_post = L^{3a+o(1)} (Proposition XK-U); T_S = L^{a+o(1)}; 1 + log₂ n = L^{o(1)}.
   299	  Total: L^{max(a + s + 2^{−2/5}c_U(τ′), a + s + max(a, σ), 3a) + o(1)}. ∎
   300	
   301	**Corollary DL25′ (arbitrary base; proved from U).** For g, h ∈ F_p^* with h ∈ ⟨g⟩, log_g h mod ord(g) in the same
   302	expected time: [DL] Corollary DL′ verbatim (random auxiliary bases t, primitive with probability ≥ 1/(1 + log₂ n),
   303	doubling budgets with Markov, every output verified, inconsistent congruences discarded).
   304	
   305	**Corollary.** For every α > 2/5, DL in F_p^* in expected L_p[α, o(1)]: L_p[2/5, c] = L_p[α, o(1)].
   306	
   307	**Lemma C25 (the DL constant is the factoring constant; proved).** Write T₁(τ) := a + s + max(a, σ) and
   308	c_U(τ) = max(2a, T₁(τ), (a + s)/(1 − 2^{−2/5})).
   309	- (i) c_DL(τ; τ) = c_U(τ) for every τ; in particular the term 3a never binds.
   310	- (ii) For all τ, τ′: c_DL(τ; τ′) ≥ min(c_U(τ), c_U(τ′)).
   311	- (iii) Hence inf_{τ,τ′} c_DL = inf_τ c_U.
   312	*Proof.* (i) ≤: (a + s) ≤ (1 − 2^{−2/5})c_U(τ), so a + s + 2^{−2/5}c_U(τ) ≤ c_U(τ); T₁ ≤ c_U(τ); and
   313	3a < a/(1 − 2^{−2/5}) ≤ (a + s)/(1 − 2^{−2/5}) ≤ c_U(τ), since 1/(1 − 2^{−2/5}) > 4.129 > 3 and s ≥ 0 (this observation is
   314	from codex dlog25_1). ≥: by (ii) with τ′ = τ. (ii): let x := c_DL(τ; τ′) and suppose x < c_U(τ′). Then
   315	a + s ≤ x − 2^{−2/5}c_U(τ′) < (1 − 2^{−2/5})x, so (a + s)/(1 − 2^{−2/5}) < x; also T₁ ≤ x and 2a < 3a ≤ x; so
   316	c_U(τ) ≤ x. (iii): (i) and (ii). ∎
   317	
