   333	## 7. Individual logarithms and the target randomisation
   334	
   335	- **Target randomisation.** T_i = g^{a_i}h^{b_i} with (a_i, b_i) uniform is uniform on F_p^* and carries an independent
   336	  uniform hidden label b_i (Lemma T_p-U). Theorem K-U's split law needs only "T uniform on F_p^*" (Lemma S_p″). The
   337	  descent geometry is Theorem U's: one Gauss split into halves ≤ √(2p), k ≍ D^{2/5} primes of size L[3/5] per half,
   338	  each descended by one special-q search into B = L[2/5]-smooth rational and algebraic values.
   339	- **Every logarithm costs a full run.** In this design there is no separate "individual-log phase": the target h enters
   340	  every row. A precomputation/descent split (rows with b = 0 precomputed once; one new row per logarithm) would need,
   341	  for the new coordinate(s), the projection of the saturated torsion lattice Λ₂ to have index coprime to n; that index is
   342	  governed by unit and class indices of K and cannot be controlled (this is exactly what Schirokauer maps or virtual logs
   343	  address, at the cost of factoring n and the conditions of [DL] §6). With ≥ C̄ + d fresh h-rows the precomputation is
   344	  useless. **Not established.** At L[2/5] this matters less than at L[1/2]: both phases would be L[2/5, Θ(1)].
   345	- What can be shared between logarithms: the field. SELECT may use targets g^a only (no h), so the kept field, the
   346	  factor-base tables and the special-q cache are target-independent and reusable (codex dlog25_1); the per-log bound
   347	  stays L_p[2/5, c_DL].
   348	
   349	## 8. Extension fields F_{p^n} in medium characteristic
   350	
   351	- **Known (rigorous).** Bender–Pomerance L_Q[1/2, √2] for p ≤ n^{o(n)}; Kleinjung–Wesolowski (pn)^{2 log₂n + O(1)}. For
   352	  Q = p^n and p = L_Q[β, c] with fixed 0 < β < 1 this is exp((2c(1 − β)/log 2 + o(1))(log Q)^β(log log Q)^{2−β})
   353	  (codex dlog25_1), i.e. L_Q[β + o(1)] but not L_Q[β, C] for a fixed C; it beats any L_Q[2/5, C] only for β < 2/5, and
   354	  is below L_Q[1/2] only for β < 1/2. For p ≥ L_Q[1/2] (medium and large characteristic) we found nothing rigorous
   355	  below L_Q[1/2] (search result).
   356	- **Does the transfer give anything? Not as it stands.**
   357	  - What transfers (fixed n, or n slowly growing): the Dixon split. Represent F_{p^n} = Z[x]/(p, φ), φ monic of
   358	    degree n irreducible mod p, and let Λ_T := {(u, v) ∈ Z[x]_{<n}² : u ≡ Tv mod (p, φ)}, of rank 2n and covolume p^n.
   359	    The analogue of Lemma S_p″'s injectivity holds when the coefficients of u, v are ≤ Y with N_{Q(φ)}(uv′ − u′v) < p^n,
   360	    since uv′ − u′v ∈ (p, φ(θ)) forces the norm to be ≥ p^n unless it is 0. So split counting over "k-products of
   361	    degree-one prime ideals" can be written.
   362	  - What fails: the halves are elements of a degree-n number field Q(φ), not integers. The rational side of the relation
   363	    supply becomes an algebraic side, and FM/LD* need Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV for smooth numbers, Drappeau Lemme 5) for
   364	    **smooth ideals in residue classes modulo ideals of Q(φ)**, plus Theorem U for factoring ideals of norm ≈ Q^{1/2}
   365	    (the shape test). Neither is available. The pair (Q(φ), K_A) would also need a common-root construction F(g₀, g₁) =
   366	    p·w over Q(φ) (a "conjugation"-type pair of fields), whose field law is not covered by Lemma IR / FM-U.
   367	  - Two simpler objections (codex dlog25_1): putting N := p^n in Theorem U's design gives arithmetic in Z/p^n, not in
   368	    F_{p^n}; and integer ratios u₀/v₀ reduce into the prime field, so they cannot represent uniform targets of F_{p^n}.
   369	    A number-field reduction onto F_{p^n} needs a prime of residue degree n, i.e. field degree ≥ n, which for n ≫ d
   370	    exceeds this design's degree budget.
   371	  - So in medium characteristic this is a research programme, not a corollary. Not pursued.
