   305	- *Proof.* By (4.1), W(x) = ±red_𝔭(ζ) with ζ ∈ μ_K, and red_𝔭 maps μ_K into μ_w(F_p). ∎
   306	
   307	**Lemma HX (hidden exponents; proved).** Let x* ∈ Z^M be primitive, i.e. gcd of its entries is 1, and a function of
   308	the round's transcript 𝒯_j (Lemma T_p). Then B_ := Σ x*_i b_i is uniform on Z/n given 𝒯_j, so Pr[gcd(B_, n) = 1 | 𝒯_j] = φ(n)/n ≥
   309	1/(1 + log₂ n).
   310	- If gcd(B_, n) = 1 and A_ := Σ x*_i a_i, then A_ + X·B_ ≡ λ (mod n), so X = (λ − A_)B_^{−1} mod n. This is exact
   311	  for every prime power of n at once, with no factorisation of n.
   312	- *Proof.*
   313	  - By Lemma T_p the b_i are i.i.d. uniform given 𝒯.
   314	  - b ↦ b·x* mod n is a surjective homomorphism, since gcd(x*) = 1 and so a Bezout vector exists. Hence the image of
   315	    the uniform law is uniform.
   316	  - log_g W(x*) = Σ x*_i(a_i + Xb_i) = A_ + X·B_ (mod n), by T_i = g^{a_i}h^{b_i}. ∎
   317	  - The bound φ(n)/n ≥ 1/(1 + log₂ n) is an elementary telescoping (as in codex dlog_1). Mertens gives
   318	    ≫ 1/log log n.
