   323	**Algorithm EXACT_DEPENDENCE and Proposition XK (proved).** On M ≥ C̄ + d ≥ C_col + d rows:
   324	1. Compute E exactly (Lemma XK0).
   325	2. Pick a random prime q ∈ [Q, 2Q] with Q := M·(log p)³. Compute by Gaussian elimination mod q a rank profile
   326	   (I, J), |I| = |J| = ρ.
   327	3. For d rows i ∉ I, solve yᵀE_{I,J} = −E_{i,J} over Q by Dixon's p-adic lifting (Dixon, Numer. Math. 40 (1982) 137–141, as usually restated: O(n³ log²(n‖A‖))), and clear
   328	   denominators: x_i := D·(y, e_i).
   329	4. Verify x_iᵀE = 0 exactly on all columns. If it fails, then rank_Q E > ρ: restart step 2.
   330	5. Apply Lemma XK4 to s = r_U + 1 of the x_i, giving n ≠ 0.
   331	6. Put x* := Σ n_j x_j / gcd. Then x* ∈ Λ₂ is primitive (Lemma XK3).
   332	7. Compute W(x*), λ (Lemma XK5) and A_, B_ (Lemma HX).
   333	
   334	**Proposition XK.** Steps 1–7 are Las Vegas. They output a primitive x* ∈ Λ₂ and λ with log_g W(x*) = λ in expected
   335	time L^{3a+o(1)} when M = BL^{o(1)} and B = L^a.
   336	
   337	*Proof.*
   338	- **Correctness.** Lemmas XK1–XK5.
   339	- **Step 2.**
   340	  - The ρ_Q × ρ_Q minor of a fixed basis has |det| ≤ ∏‖rows‖ ≤ (O(k log p))^{1.5ρ}. Each row has ≤ 2k(1 + log₂p)
   341	    nonzeros, each O(k log p).
   342	  - So at most O(ρ log log p) primes divide it. [Q, 2Q] contains ≫ M(log p)²/log M ≫ ρ(log p) primes, so
   343	    Pr[rank mod q < rank_Q] ≤ 1/2.
   344	  - A nonsingular minor mod q is nonsingular over Q, so ρ ≤ rank_Q.
   345	  - Step 4 does *not* certify ρ = rank_Q (codex dlog_2 #4). The chosen d rows may already lie in the rational span of
   346	    the rows in I.
   347	  - That does no harm. If verification passes, the d vectors are valid kernel vectors, independent through their free
   348	    coordinates e_i, which is all XK4 needs. If it fails, resample q.
   349	  - A q that preserves a fixed maximal minor (Pr ≥ 1/2) guarantees that verification passes, so the expected number of
   350	    restarts is ≤ 2.
   351	  - Cost O(M·C_col·ρ) operations mod q = L^{3a+o(1)}.
   352	- **Step 3.**
   353	  - Dixon: one Q-inverse mod a prime (ρ³), then O(ρ log(ρ‖E‖)) lifting steps of O(ρ²) each, for each of d right-hand
   354	    sides. Total Õ(ρ³) = L^{3a+o(1)}.
   355	  - The outputs have β_x = O(ρ log log p) = L^{a+o(1)} bits (Cramer/Hadamard).
   356	- **Steps 4–7.**
   357	  - Step 4: nnz(E)·β_x = L^{2a+o(1)}.
   358	  - Step 5: Lemma XK4 with β_x = L^{a+o(1)} costs L^{2a+o(1)}.
   359	  - Step 7: M modular exponentiations with L^{a+o(1)}-bit exponents cost L^{2a+o(1)}.
   360	  - Lemma XK0(iii): Õ(B) + poly(log p).
   361	- ∎
   362	
   363	Remarks.
   364	- (i) **C_LA = 3** comes from dense elimination. A sparse rank profile plus sparse rational solving would give smaller
   365	  C_LA, but rigorous sparse integer solvers (EGGSV 2006) rely on an unproved block-projection conjecture, so we do not
   366	  use them. Only C_LA ≤ 1 + 1/θ matters for the constants.
   367	- (ii) **Black-box alternative.** Ge / Lenstra–Silverberg Thm 1.11(i) computes generators of the multiplicative
   368	  relation lattice of the explicit row elements in polynomial time with unspecified exponent. This is the route of r19
   369	  §2 and dlog_1 §4. It gives Theorem DL with 3a replaced by Ca, and C unquantified.
   370	- (iii) **Factoring.** The same device gives a character-free replacement for LV6 in Theorem A (also noted in r19 §2).
   371	  - Take Λ₂′ := {x ∈ Λ₂ : γ_x ∈ μ_K², sign product +1}, a sublattice of index ≤ 4.
   372	  - Its reduction mod 2 has dimension ≥ rank Λ₂ − 2, so with M ≥ C_col + d + 2 there is x with some odd x_i.
   373	  - Then (∏r_i^{x_i})² ≡ ψ(ζ′)² with ζ′ ∈ μ_K computable, and DD applies.
   374	  - For composite N, ψ must be well defined on the group generated by the a − bα. It is, because Z[α] ≅ Z[x]/(f) for
   375	    primitive f (Gauss's lemma), and one localises at the elements whose ψ-images are units, all of which are
   376	    gcd-checked.
   377	  - Cost L^{3a} instead of L^{2a}. That is harmless for θ ≤ 1/2, i.e. whenever c < 1.
   378	  - It would remove Lagarias–Odlyzko, Stark/Landau and the Hensel root from Theorem A.
   379	  - Not worked out further here (lv6's area).
   380	  - **Algorithmic sketch: existence proved, cost not established.** XK outputs one primitive torsion relation. The
   381	    factoring variant needs enough independent torsion relations, the saturation of their span, and the two sign
   382	    constraints imposed within budget (codex dlog_2 #12).
   383	
   384	## 5. Theorem DL, cost and constants (proved from A)
   385	
   386	**Theorem DL.** Fix admissible (d, k, μ, η₀) with θ < 1. DLOG is Las Vegas and has expected time
   387	  L_p[1/2, c_DL(θ) + o(1)],  c_DL(θ) := min_{a>0} max(a + 1/√2, a + θ/(2a), 3a),
   388	uniformly for primes p ≥ p₀(d, k, μ, η₀). For θ ≤ 1/2, c_DL = (1 + θ)/√2, attained at a = θ/√2.
   389	
   390	*Proof.*

