   202	**The recursion inequality.** Put F(x) := sup_{2≤n≤x} E[cost of FACTOR_rec[a](n)]. F is nondecreasing.
   203	- **Finiteness.** F(x) < ∞ for every x, by strong induction on n.
   204	  - With the alternation wrapper (§1.1, item 3), every node's cost is ≤ 2√n + O(1) whatever happens in the core, so
   205	    F(x) ≤ 2 log₂x·(2√x + O(1)) < ∞ outright.
   206	  - For n ≥ N₀ (a threshold that need only exist) the inequality below holds with finite P(n) and D(n), and it calls
   207	    F only at √(2n) < n.
   208	- **Accounting.** FACTOR(N) builds a factor tree with at most 2Ω(N) − 1 ≤ 2 log₂N nodes.
   209	  - Each node n | N costs O(L_n^{a+o(1)}) for AKS, perfect-power detection and trial division to B(n), plus one
   210	    SPLIT^O(n′) on its cofactor.
   211	  - Node values are random, but each node's conditional expected cost given its value is at most the supremum over
   212	    n ≤ N.
   213	  - The O-calls inside SPLIT^O(n′) are FACTOR_rec[a] on inputs ≤ √(2n′) ≤ √(2N), with fresh coins. Their conditional
   214	    expected cost is ≤ F(√(2N)).
   215	- By Lemma W and (A1)–(A2), for x ≥ x₀:
   216	  **F(x) ≤ 2 log₂x·[P(x) + 2D(x)·F(√(2x))],** with P(x) = L_x^{max(2a, a+θ/(2a)) + o(1)} and D(x) = L_x^{a+o(1)}.
