   425	**Corollary DL′ (arbitrary base; proved from A, following codex dlog_1 §4).** For g, h ∈ F_p^* with h ∈ ⟨g⟩, log_g h
   426	mod ord(g) can be computed in the same expected time.
   427	- *Proof.* Draw a random auxiliary base t.
   428	  - A primitive root occurs with probability φ(n)/n ≥ 1/(1 + log₂ n).
   429	  - Run DLOG(p, t, g) and DLOG(p, t, h) under a time budget that is doubled each stage, with c·(1 + log₂ n) fresh t's
   430	    per stage. The fixed c is chosen so that a stage whose budget exceeds 4× the expected primitive-root time fails
   431	    with probability ≤ 1/4.
   432	  - For primitive t and budget ≥ 4·E[time], Markov's inequality gives each of the two runs failure probability
   433	    ≤ 1/4, so both succeed with probability ≥ 1/2.
   434	  - Since stage failure is ≤ 1/4 < 1/2 once the budget is large enough, the doubling series converges.
   435	  - Every output is verified (t^u = g, t^v = h). Then solve u·x ≡ v (mod n).
   436	    - If t is primitive, this is consistent because h ∈ ⟨g⟩.
   437	    - If t is non-primitive and both runs still return verified exponents, it may be inconsistent. If gcd(u, n) ∤ v,
   438	      discard and continue.
   439	    - Any solution x is checked against g^x = h before output.
   440	  - For a non-primitive t, DLOG may fail to terminate within budget, but it never returns an unverified answer.
   441	- Expected total: L^{c_DL + o(1)}. No factorisation of p − 1 is needed.
   442	- Alternatively, factor p − 1 with Theorem A at L^{(1+θ)/√2} ≤ L^{c_DL}. That route uses Theorem A's Steps 8–10
   443	  (LV6, DD) as well, so it depends on more of the internal chain than Theorem DL does. ∎
